  "circularity": "scoring an LLM against a gold two other LLMs wrote is not an independent test. It is bounded rather than denied: the annotators are from a different model family, and every arm is also scored on the subset where BOTH annotators agreed \u2014 where they disagreed there is no fact for a third model to get right.",
  "counts_note": "gold_positives is the denominator of recall and predicted_positives the denominator of precision; the ratio between them is the over-calling this comparison is largely about. They do not sum to n_items because dimensions co-occur and most spans carry none.",
  "gold_positives": {
    "family": 4,
    "historical": 38,
    "negated": 34,
    "uncertain": 37,
    "unmodified": 290
  },
  "n_annotator_consensus": 381,
  "n_items": 400,
  "n_unmodified": 290,
  "not_a_candidate_detector": "an API call is not replayable, not offline and not hashable into an operator version, and this package promises deterministic edit scripts. These arms price the gap between the shipped detector and the ceiling. A cheap model that scores well is an argument for DISTILLING it into an encoder, not for calling it at runtime.",
  "question": "Do cheap LLMs reach the same assertion judgements as frontier ones?",
  "split": "held_out",
  "why": "the gold is two frontier models agreeing at kappa 0.845-0.913, so that tier is already measured. What is unknown is where the price/agreement curve bends."
}
